IMDB Movie Data Analysis Using SQL

Project Overview

This project focuses on analyzing an IMDB movie dataset using SQL.

The objective is to extract meaningful insights about movies, genres, ratings, production companies, directors and actors.

The project contains a series of SQL queries that answer business-oriented questions using different SQL concepts such as filtering, aggregation, joins, Common Table Expressions, window functions, ranking, conditional logic and date functions.

The analysis is performed on the IMDB database.

Database Tables

The project uses the following tables:

1. movie
2. genre
3. ratings
4. names
5. role_mapping
6. director_mapping

Key Analysis Areas

1. Movie production trends
2. Genre analysis
3. Movie duration analysis
4. Movie ratings
5. Production company analysis
6. Director analysis
7. Actor and actress analysis
8. Movie popularity based on votes
9. Worldwide gross income
10. Previous and current movie performance
11. Running totals and moving averages
12. Ranking and classification of movies

SQL Concepts Used

1. SELECT
2. WHERE
3. GROUP BY
4. HAVING
5. ORDER BY
6. DISTINCT
7. COUNT
8. SUM
9. AVG
10. MIN
11. MAX
12. ROUND
13. CASE WHEN
14. LIKE
15. BETWEEN
16. INNER JOIN
17. Common Table Expressions (CTEs)
18. Subqueries
19. RANK()
20. DENSE_RANK()
21. ROW_NUMBER()
22. LAG()
23. PARTITION BY
24. Aggregate functions
25. Date functions
26. Conditional aggregation
27. Weighted averages

Project Questions and Analysis

1. Database Exploration

The project begins by finding the total number of rows in each table.

Tables analyzed:

1. director_mapping
2. genre
3. movie
4. names
5. ratings
6. role_mapping

This provides an initial understanding of the size of each table.

2. Missing Value Analysis

The movie table was analyzed to identify columns containing NULL values.

The analysis covered:

1. title
2. year
3. date_published
4. duration
5. country
6. worldwide gross income
7. languages
8. production company

Conditional aggregation using CASE WHEN was used to count NULL values.

3. Movie Production by Year

The number of movies released in each year was calculated using GROUP BY.

This was followed by a month-wise analysis to understand seasonal movie production trends.

MONTH() was used to extract the month from the date_published column.

4. Movies Produced in USA and India

The number of movies produced in the USA or India during 2019 was calculated.

The LIKE operator was used to identify countries containing USA or India.

5. Genre Analysis

The unique genres available in the dataset were identified using DISTINCT.

The number of movies produced in each genre was then calculated by joining the movie and genre tables.

6. Most Frequently Produced Genre

Movies were grouped by genre and counted to identify the genres with the highest number of movies.

Drama was identified as one of the most frequently produced genres.

7. Single-Genre Movies

A subquery was used to identify movies associated with exactly one genre.

GROUP BY and HAVING COUNT() were used to determine movies having only one genre.

8. Average Movie Duration by Genre

The average movie duration was calculated for each genre.

The movie and genre tables were joined and AVG() was used to calculate the average duration.

The results were rounded to make the output easier to interpret.

9. Thriller Genre Ranking

The thriller genre was ranked against other genres based on the number of movies produced.

A Common Table Expression was created and RANK() was used to calculate the genre ranking.

10. Ratings Table Analysis

The minimum and maximum values of the following ratings columns were identified:

1. Average rating
2. Total votes
3. Median rating

This provided an overview of the range of values present in the ratings table.

11. Top Movies by Average Rating

Movies were ranked based on their average rating.

DENSE_RANK() and ROW_NUMBER() were used to explore ranking behavior when multiple movies have the same rating.

The analysis also considered movies sharing the same rank.

12. Movie Count by Median Rating

Movies were grouped by median rating to determine the number of movies associated with each median rating.

The results were ordered by median rating.

13. Production Houses with Highly Rated Movies

Production companies were analyzed based on the number of movies having an average rating greater than 8.

Production companies were ranked using a window function.

14. Movies Released in March 2017

The number of movies released in March 2017 in the USA was calculated for each genre.

Only movies with more than 1,000 votes were considered.

This analysis combined:

1. Date filtering
2. Country filtering
3. Genre grouping
4. Rating information
5. Vote thresholds

15. Movies Starting With "The"

Movies whose titles start with "The" and have an average rating greater than 8 were identified.

The movie, rating and genre were displayed.

The LIKE operator was used for title filtering.

16. Movies With Median Rating of 8

The number of movies released between 1 April 2018 and 1 April 2019 with a median rating of 8 was calculated.

The BETWEEN operator was used for date filtering.

17. German vs Italian Movies

The total number of votes received by German and Italian movies was compared.

Conditional aggregation using CASE WHEN was used to calculate the total votes for both languages.

18. NULL Value Analysis of Names Table

The names table was analyzed for NULL values in:

1. Name
2. Height
3. Date of birth
4. Known-for movies

Conditional aggregation was used to count NULL values.

19. Top Directors in Top Genres

The top three genres were first identified based on the number of highly rated movies.

The top three directors within each of those genres were then identified.

This analysis used:

1. CTEs
2. JOINs
3. GROUP BY
4. RANK()
5. PARTITION BY

20. Top Actors Based on Highly Rated Movies

Actors whose movies had a median rating of at least 8 were identified.

The actors were ranked based on the number of such movies.

21. Top Production Houses by Total Votes

Production companies were ranked according to the total number of votes received by their movies.

SUM() was used to calculate the total votes.

RANK() was then used to rank the production companies.

22. Ranking Indian Actors

Indian actors were analyzed based on their movie performance.

The analysis considered only actors who had acted in at least five Indian movies.

A weighted average rating was calculated using:

Average Rating × Total Votes

The weighted average was then divided by total votes.

Actors were ranked using their weighted average rating, with total votes used as a tie breaker.

23. Ranking Indian Hindi Actresses

The top five actresses in Hindi movies released in India were identified.

Only actresses who had acted in at least three Indian movies were considered.

A weighted average rating based on total votes was calculated.

The actresses were ranked using:

1. Weighted average rating
2. Total votes as a tie breaker

24. Thriller Movie Classification

Thriller movies with at least 25,000 votes were classified based on average rating.

The CASE statement was used
